WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.099
"Vitamin E Supplements for Cancer, 
immunity, and Longevity"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.160
Randomized controlled trials

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.640
show that daily
vitamin D supplementation

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.880
appears to reduce the risk
of acute respiratory infections

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.840
in children and adolescents,

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.320
but it does not seem
to make a difference in adults,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.680
nor does D appear effective

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.240
for boosting
antibody responses

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.600
to influenza vaccination.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.960
What about vitamin E?

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.160
Most places, you'll read

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.480
that there are eight different
tocopherols and tocotrienols

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.600
that are collectively known
as vitamin E,

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.960
but only alpha-tocopherol
has been shown

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.240
to protect against
human vitamin E deficiency.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.680
So alpha-tocopherol
is the only bona fide vitamin E

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.640
and is the one found
in vitamin E supplements.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.640
There are observational data
suggesting

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.440
those self-reporting
supplementation with vitamin E

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.240
have, for example,
better pneumonia outcomes,

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:58.240
but only interventional trials
can prove if this is the case.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.920
Compared to placebo supplements,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.640
vitamin E was able
to significantly boost immunity

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.520
to hepatitis B
and tetanus vaccinations

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.720
(though not to diphtheria
or pneumonia).

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.200
One randomized controlled trial

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.840
found that vitamin E
supplementation of the elderly

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.720
did not cut down on lower
respiratory tract infections

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.280
(like pneumonia)
but did reduce the incidence

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.240
of upper respiratory tract
infections

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.560
(like the common cold)

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.720
by about 20 percent
over one year.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.040
But another study
using the same dose

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.200
found no preventive benefit,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.120
and vitamin E supplementation
actually made the infections

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.520
they did get even worse,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.200
with a greater number
and severity of symptoms,

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.280
including a greater likelihood
of fever

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.240
and activity restriction.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.400
By far the largest study
on vitamin E

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.920
and respiratory infections

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.600
was the ATBC Study
that randomized

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.360
nearly 15,000 smokers
to vitamin E or placebo

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.280
for about 5 years.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.680
No overall effect
was found either way

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.880
between the vitamin E
and placebo groups,

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.120
but upon subgroup analysis

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.040
it appeared
that while lighter smokers

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.920
living in cities
appeared to benefit

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.480
with about a 50 percent
lower risk

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.920
of catching the common cold,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.240
vitamin E appeared
to be a detriment

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.600
to heavier smokers
living outside of cities,

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.080
resulting in about a 50 percent
increased risk.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.200
"Accordingly,"
concluded the investigators,

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.640
"caution should be maintained

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:19.920
in public health
recommendations

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.320
on vitamin E supplementation

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.040
until its effects
are better understood."

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.200
The pneumonia results
from the same study

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.920
took a similar turn.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.080
No overall effect of vitamin E
compared to placebo,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.640
but upon further analysis,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.600
vitamin E appeared
to decrease pneumonia risk

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.880
among lighter smokers
who exercised,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.640
yet increased pneumonia risk

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.120
among heavier smokers
who did not.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.880
Higher pneumonia rates
were also noted

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.520
in those randomized
to vitamin D

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.520
and were either low
or high body weight,

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.400
but only, apparently,
among those

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.200
with higher dietary
vitamin C intake.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.800
Heavy smokers
who got a lot of C

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.720
also seemed to have
higher tuberculosis rates on E

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.600
compared to placebo.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.760
This kind of slicing
and dicing of data

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.960
is frowned upon, as it can
spawn spurious connections,

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:11.040
so these findings are regarded
only as exploratory

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.080
for further
research directions.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.720
Overall mortality
in the ATBC Study was similar

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.160
between the vitamin E
and placebo groups,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.760
though again
there were subgroups

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.120
that appeared to have
their lives either cut short

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.640
or extended.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.320
There have been dozens
of controlled clinical trials

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:29.280
on vitamin E,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.640
and most meta-analyses
found a small

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.040
but apparent real increase
in the risk of mortality

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.240
among those randomized
to vitamin E.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.560
In other words, those who buy
vitamin E supplements

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.600
may be, in effect, paying
to live a shorter life.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.200
Across 46 trials,
12 percent died

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.760
in the vitamin E groups

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.320
versus about 10 percent
in the placebo groups.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.200
This is similar to the increase
in mortality

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.440
seen with beta carotene
supplementation-

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.040
approximately 14 percent
versus 11 percent

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.160
across 26 randomized trials.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.680
The goal of the ATBC

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:09.040
was to see if taking
antioxidant supplements

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.080
like vitamin E or beta carotene

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:13.960
could help prevent lung cancer
in smokers.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.400
Vitamin E had no effect
on lung cancer,

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.360
and beta-carotene unexpectedly
actually increased

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:20.920
lung cancer rates,

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:22.960
but vitamin E did,
incidentally,

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.520
appear to reduce
incident prostate cancer rates.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.240
This helped inspire
the SELECT study,

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:33.520
the largest cancer
prevention trial ever performed.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.800
More than 35,000 men were
randomized to take vitamin E,

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.080
selenium, both,
or neither (just placebos).

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.040
The selenium
didn't appear to matter,

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.520
but those randomized
to the straight vitamin E group

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.240
ended up with a 17 percent
greater risk

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.240
of developing prostate cancer.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.040
If 100 men took vitamin E
for a decade,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.720
we would expect to see
one or two of them

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.520
to be diagnosed
with prostate cancer

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.640
that they otherwise
wouldn't have gotten

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:18.840
had they wasted their money
on sugar pills instead.

